### What happens when you expose a JPA entity directly?

When a controller returns a JPA entity, Jackson serializes every readable field into JSON. This means sensitive columns like `passwordHash`, `internalRiskScore`, soft-delete flags, and audit metadata are all sent to the client. You can try to hide fields with `@JsonIgnore`, but that scatters serialization concerns across the entity and is easy to get wrong when a new sensitive column is added.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
public class Customer {
    @Id private String id;
    private String name;
    private String passwordHash;       // leaks if entity is returned
    private int fraudRiskScore;        // internal only
    private BigDecimal balance;
}

// Controller returns entity directly – all fields exposed:
@GetMapping("/customers/{id}")
public Customer getCustomer(@PathVariable String id) {
    return customerRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();
}
```

```text
// Response includes secrets:
{ "id": "C001", "name": "Rajesh", "passwordHash": "$2a$10$xK...",
  "fraudRiskScore": 78, "balance": 150000.00 }
```

### How does entity exposure couple the schema to the API?

The entity shape mirrors the table. When you rename a column, split a table, or change a type for internal reasons, the serialized entity changes and any client parsing that shape breaks. A DTO is the stable contract that absorbs schema changes behind a mapping layer.

```java
// Before: entity has field "id"
// After schema migration: field renamed to "accountNumber"
// Every client that parsed "id" from the JSON now breaks.

// With a DTO – the mapping absorbs the change:
public record CustomerResponse(String customerId, String name, BigDecimal balance) {}

public CustomerResponse toResponse(Customer c) {
    return new CustomerResponse(c.getAccountNumber(), c.getName(), c.getBalance());
    // DTO field stays "customerId" regardless of the entity field name
}
```

### What is LazyInitializationException and when does it happen?

Entities often have lazy relationships that load only when accessed within an open persistence context. If the controller returns the entity and Jackson runs after the transaction has closed, touching a lazy collection throws `LazyInitializationException`, producing a 500 error.

```java
@Entity
public class Account {
    @Id private String id;
    private BigDecimal balance;

    @OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, mappedBy = "account")
    private List<Transaction> transactions;   // loads only on access
}

// Jackson touches transactions after session closed → crash:
@GetMapping("/accounts/{id}")
public Account get(@PathVariable String id) {
    return accountRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    // LazyInitializationException when serializer hits transactions
}
```

Mapping to a DTO inside the service transaction avoids this because the serializer never touches the entity's lazy relationships.

```java
// Correct – map inside the transaction:
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public AccountResponse getAccount(String id) {
    Account a = accountRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    return new AccountResponse(a.getId(), a.getBalance()); // no lazy field touched
}
```

### How does entity exposure block API versioning?

A public API often needs multiple versions. If the entity is the response, you cannot have two shapes because there is one entity. With DTOs you create `AccountResponseV1` and `AccountResponseV2` mapping from the same entity.

```java
// v1 – original contract:
public record AccountResponseV1(String id, BigDecimal balance) {}

// v2 – adds available balance:
public record AccountResponseV2(
        String id, BigDecimal balance, BigDecimal availableBalance) {}

// Both map from the same entity:
@GetMapping("/api/v1/accounts/{id}")
public AccountResponseV1 getV1(@PathVariable String id) {
    Account a = service.findById(id);
    return new AccountResponseV1(a.getId(), a.getBalance());
}

@GetMapping("/api/v2/accounts/{id}")
public AccountResponseV2 getV2(@PathVariable String id) {
    Account a = service.findById(id);
    return new AccountResponseV2(a.getId(), a.getBalance(),
            a.getBalance().subtract(a.getPendingHolds()));
}
```

### What is mass-assignment and how does it relate?

Binding a request body directly to an entity enables mass-assignment, where a client sets a field it should not, like `isAdmin` or `balance`. A request DTO without those fields blocks this attack.

```java
// Wrong – entity binding allows mass-assignment:
@PostMapping("/customers")
public Customer create(@RequestBody Customer customer) {
    // A client can send {"name":"Rajesh", "fraudRiskScore":0, "balance":999999}
    return customerRepository.save(customer);
}

// Correct – request DTO has only allowed fields:
public record CreateCustomerRequest(
        @NotBlank String name,
        @NotBlank String password
) {}

@PostMapping("/customers")
public CustomerResponse create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateCustomerRequest req) {
    return customerService.create(req); // service controls which fields are set
}
```

### How do you fix all these problems?

The fix is a clean mapping at the service boundary. Request DTOs for input, response DTOs for output, and the entity stays below the service.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dto.*;
import com.bank.model.Customer;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class CustomerService {

    @Transactional
    public CustomerResponse create(CreateCustomerRequest req) {
        Customer entity = new Customer();
        entity.setName(req.name());
        entity.setPasswordHash(hashPassword(req.password()));
        entity.setFraudRiskScore(50); // server-controlled default
        customerRepo.save(entity);
        return toResponse(entity);
    }

    private CustomerResponse toResponse(Customer c) {
        return new CustomerResponse(c.getId(), c.getName(), c.getBalance());
    }
}
```

### What about @JsonIgnore as an alternative?

`@JsonIgnore` is tempting but insufficient. It hides specific fields from serialization, but it is fragile: every new sensitive column must be annotated, and forgetting one leaks it. It also conflates persistence and serialization concerns in one class. A DTO flips the default: only fields you explicitly include are exposed.

```java
// Fragile – must remember @JsonIgnore on every sensitive field:
@Entity
public class Customer {
    @Id private String id;
    private String name;
    @JsonIgnore private String passwordHash;   // must not forget this
    @JsonIgnore private int fraudRiskScore;    // or this
    private BigDecimal balance;
    // If someone adds a field "ssn" and forgets @JsonIgnore – it leaks.
}

// Safe – DTO only includes what you choose:
public record CustomerResponse(String id, String name, BigDecimal balance) {}
// No passwordHash, no fraudRiskScore, no ssn – they simply do not exist here.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The DTO boundary is not boilerplate for its own sake. It is the single line that keeps security, schema evolution, concurrency correctness, and versioning all independent. Tools like MapStruct generate the mapping at compile time, so the common objection of too much boilerplate does not hold. MapStruct also catches unmapped fields at build time: if someone adds a new entity field, the unmapped-source warning forces a conscious decision rather than an accidental leak.

In a banking context, this boundary is also where regulatory compliance decisions live. Every field in the response DTO is an explicit choice to expose data, and every field absent from the request DTO is an explicit choice to block client input.
